# Replace complete paragraphs by identifying their opening; preserve equations exactly.
def para(start,new):
 global s
 blocks=s.split('\n\n'); hits=[i for i,b in enumerate(blocks) if b.startswith(start)]; assert len(hits)==1,(start,hits);blocks[hits[0]]=new;s='\n\n'.join(blocks)
para('A position reference can induce', 'A geometrically clear waypoint can induce an unsafe sailing trajectory because a recurrent controller responds through wind-dependent dynamics and control history. We adapt control-induced motion prediction to candidate position references together with their hold-and-restore protocols. Independent instances of an inherited sailing policy and dynamics reconstruct causal history, fit local wind from motion and compare synchronized clearance before reference delivery. In a frozen 24-task simulation comparison, reactive navigation, a calibrated low-order predictor and detailed prediction safely complete 19, 23 and 24 tasks. Their counts are 3, 7 and 8 in eight combined-pressure encounters; all three complete the other sixteen tasks. Detailed prediction gains one completion over the low-order comparator, while adding 7 s on average and up to 308 s across 23 common-safe pairs, with 2133.23 versus 378.44 s cumulative episode CPU. Fixed-protocol diagnostics explain a local fidelity advantage and retain a 106 m adverse prediction error. The contribution is an execution-consistent supervisory interface and evidence of its conditional avoidance value and cost under ideal computation, where planning pauses physical time.')
para('We adapt the GVO principle', 'The missing interface is a candidate execution protocol, rather than only a waypoint coordinate. For a recurrent controller, changing the reference also changes its local frame and reconstructed memory; holding that reference and later restoring route feedback changes the subsequent trajectory. We adapt the control-induced prediction principle of GVO to these finite protocols. A motion-based local wind fit supplies the branch model; MoWe [4] motivates motion-based wind estimation, while the bounded inverse-dynamics fit used here is a separate implementation.')
needle='## II. System and Evaluation Conditions'
s=s.replace(needle, 'Our contribution is an execution-consistent supervisory interface and a matched empirical assessment. The interface preserves the pending old-reference action, causal policy history, hold duration and route-manager restoration in each branch. The inherited policy, dynamics and established prediction principle remain separate from this adaptation; state reconstruction and the clearance-error inequality are not proposed as new control theory. The assessment tests whether detailed execution modeling adds value over a specified calibrated low-order predictor, and accounts for intervention and computation costs alongside safe completions. In the primary comparison, its additional completion occurs in a combined-pressure encounter; an endpoint encounter instead incurs a 308 s detour. These complementary results define the scope of the method.\n\n'+needle)
para('The implemented four-degree', 'The inherited four-degree dynamics include position, roll, heading, surge/sway velocities, roll/yaw rates and actual sail/rudder angles. The model lineage is Xiao and Jouffroy [8]; attribution to the supplied research codebase [9] is separate. Independent predictor and execution instances use the same model structure. Supplement S9 and S11–S12 identify exact parameters, observation mapping, source/weight hashes and reproduction commands. The journal\'s 14D formulation and this 18D asset are not treated as identical checkpoints; the exact original training-run linkage is undocumented.')
para('An active reference g defines', 'An active reference g defines a rigid local frame with angle θ equal to its bearing at application and origin g − 500[cos θ,sin θ], placing the reference at (500,0) m without rescaling. The first 14 inputs describe local pose, body velocities/rates, actual actuator angles, progress and reference coordinates. Two obstacle distance/bearing pairs use max(0,d−R+40 m), with exclusion radius R, a 150 m encoded-distance cutoff, distance/bearing sorting and zero-filled missing pairs. This fixed input adapter is shared by all comparators. Its numerical distance transformation is not claimed as an independently established contribution; a historical matched input check did not establish its necessity.')
para('The real nominal relay is called', 'Each branch clones the route manager after its current nominal call. While a non-nominal reference is held, the real manager is not advanced; release clears its incumbent before restoring feedback. Branches use the same rules. Thus candidate predictions specify how a reference is applied and released, rather than interpreting its bearing as an instantaneous heading. Supplement S2 records exact reconstruction and clock handling.')
para('Each ĉ uses the synchronized', 'Each ĉ uses the synchronized segment expression and the same obstacle-specific D_j. The effective horizon Kγ≤60 includes every completed interval up to predicted arrival or termination; early arrival is valid but does not certify later occupancy. Failed route or integration predictions are excluded. P=15 s is the hold/review period; ρ=30 m is an extra predicted margin above the unchanged true exclusion boundary. Let V contain valid integration-successful candidates, F(ρ)={γ∈V:m̂(γ)≥ρ}, n denote nominal, and ι denote enumeration order. The terminal preference is')
s=s.replace('### A. Historical evidence and targeted revision','### A. Paired tasks and information conditions')
para('Historical R8 evidence comprises', 'The primary evaluation comprises 24 frozen one-kilometer instances: eight combined-pressure encounters and four each of ordinary crossing, overtaking, endpoint occupation and non-conflict. All methods were frozen before outcomes were observed, and all generated tasks and failures are retained. Earlier short/long tests, development checks and a separate twelve-task confirmation are supporting evidence with their own denominators in Supplement S6; overlapping mechanism cases are not pooled.')
para('All targets in these main tests', 'Targets in the primary evaluation move at constant world-frame velocity; the extrapolation model is therefore matched. Base wind speed is 8 m/s. Twelve tasks use stable wind and twelve use fast spatial/temporal variation: direction waves of 45 degrees, perturbations up to 6 degrees and speed modulation of 20 percent. Historical profiles and exact realized values are indexed in the supplement. Common-safe time/path differences exclude pairs where either method fails, and task—not prediction window—is the sampling unit.')
para('The new comparator L retains','The comparator L retains the same supervisor, candidates, H/P/ρ, cost, fallback, endpoint manager and real execution. Only the predictor is replaced. Its low-order state is position, course χ and course rate r, initialized from causal recent position differences. The last observed ground speed v stays fixed over the horizon. For bearing error e to the currently active reference, it uses')
para('Table I is the primary comparison', 'Table I reports all 24 frozen confirmation tasks. Gains/losses are paired changes in safe completion. Time differences condition on both methods succeeding; they do not assign an artificial navigation time to failures. Table II stratifies the same tasks by their prespecified families.')
insert='''Table II. Safe completions by prespecified family. Pressure changes geometry, wind and static context jointly.

| Family | n | R | L | S15 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Combined pressure | 8 | 3 | 7 | 8 |
| Crossing | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| Overtaking | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| Endpoint | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| Non-conflict | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| Total | 24 | 19 | 23 | 24 |

'''
s=s.replace('Against L, S15 gains',insert+'Against L, S15 gains',1)
para('Against L, S15 gains', 'Against L, S15 gains one safe completion and loses none. Their 23 common-safe pairs have mean S15−L time +7 s, median 0 and maximum +308 s. Episode CPU is 2133.23 s for S15 versus 378.44 s for L. All five R failures and the one L failure occur in the combined-pressure family; all methods safely complete the other sixteen tasks. L reaches the goal on its failed task, but its buffer incursion makes that task unsafe. S15 and L each activate on eleven tasks. These results show one additional completion within this designed envelope, with navigation and computation costs; they neither isolate a single pressure factor nor estimate natural traffic benefit.')
s=s.replace('The unique L-unsafe/S15-safe task is r12new_mixed_pressure_1.', 'The unique L-unsafe/S15-safe task is the second combined-pressure instance (Supplement S8).').replace('The strongest new time-cost counterexample is r12new_endpoint_3:', 'The largest time-cost counterexample is the fourth endpoint instance:')
para('Historical R8 evidence rescues', 'Separate historical evaluation recovered two reactive failures among thirty short tasks and one among four long tasks. Another twelve-task confirmation produced no additional completion over R and included a 271 s endpoint detour. Supplement S6 preserves these groups and the overlap of development cases. They do not enlarge the independent task count in Table I.')
s=s.replace('Table II shows all four guards','Table III shows all four guards').replace('Table II. Guard sensitivity','Table III. Guard sensitivity')
para('The targeted revision supports', 'The matched experiment locates the additional benefit of detailed prediction in the combined-pressure family: 8/8 safe completions versus 7/8 for L and 3/8 for R, with 16/16 for all methods elsewhere. The single gain over L coexists with +7 s mean and +308 s worst additional time on common-safe tasks and higher episode CPU. L therefore remains a practical computational alternative within the evaluated information setting; the comparison does not establish that detailed dynamics are universally necessary. In particular, L was calibrated on unchanged-reference 15 s windows, not all 60 s hold-and-restore maneuvers. The fixed-protocol checks support local decision fidelity, while the 106 m adverse error and best-effort fallback bound the interpretation of the empirical guard.')
para('The evaluation assumes', 'The results apply to the inherited dynamics, noiseless current motion observations, a known static map and one low-speed constant-velocity target. Maneuvering targets, model mismatch, multi-vessel rules and full COLREGs behavior remain outside the tested scope. Planning pauses physical time, and a measured detailed call can exceed the 15 s review period; deployment needs a moving-while-computing protocol and its own evaluation. Within this scope, comparing executable reference protocols provides a concrete way to expose both useful avoidance choices and costly interventions before selecting a more complex predictor for an application.')
